monetization_data.faq_schema:
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://kubaik.github.io/why-leader-election-fails-on-cloud-networks/#faq",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does my leader keep changing every few minutes?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Frequent leader changes usually indicate that the lease is expiring prematurely. Common causes include network latency spikes causing missed heartbeats, GC pauses on the leader, or clock skew causing the leader to think its lease is still valid while followers disagree. Check the coordination service logs for lease expiration events and correlate with network and GC metrics. Increasing the lease TTL can help, but first fix the underlying cause."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I use a database as a lock service for leader election?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Using a relational database with a table and row-level locking is possible but risky. Databases are not designed for high-frequency lease renewals, and they may not provide linearizable reads under all isolation levels. For example, PostgreSQL's default isolation level (Read Committed) does not prevent phantom reads, and you need to use SELECT ... FOR UPDATE carefully. If the database is partitioned, you may end up with two leaders. It's better to use a dedicated coordination service."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I test leader election under network partitions?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Use a tool like tc (traffic control) on Linux to introduce network delays and packet loss, or use a chaos engineering tool like Chaos Mesh to simulate partitions. Run your cluster with three or five nodes and introduce a partition between the leader and the followers. Verify that a new leader is elected and that the old leader stops acting as leader within the lease TTL. Also test clock skew by adjusting the system clock on one node (in a test environment) and observing the behavior."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a fencing token and why do I need it?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A fencing token is a monotonically increasing number that is included with every write request. When a new leader is elected, it increments the token. Downstream systems (like databases or storage services) reject any write with a token lower than the highest seen. This prevents an old leader that is still running (e.g., due to a long GC pause) from making writes after a new leader has taken over. Without fencing tokens, you can have split brain even with a lease-based election."
      }
    }
  ]
}
